Subject:  Re: Ethics For Students on Net

This is one UHH is beginning to wrestle with!  When (not if) we find
"hackers", a broadly defined term here, we eliminate their accounts!  The
CS dept instructors require no less computer homework, they simply MUST
find their own computer to do it on. We hold a formal hearing with the
culprits prior to reinstatement (if they get reinstated at all!). Formal
hearing members are me (the techie), the CS dept chair and the Natural
Sciences Division Chair and perhaps this year the Dean of the College of
Arts and Sciences!.  A real, academic type standing probationary period is
established for the culprit through this hearing. The last one resulted in
giving the student Novell access only removing their Unix access
permanently. (We caught this guy attempting to break my supervisor password on
the machine I am at now!)

Use of State equipement for classwork is a priviledge, not a right! I have
revoked the restored these priviledges several times for people doing just
as you described below, giving out their account passwords to friends. The
friendships seem to crumble though when the central person loses his/her
access. I also am VERY strict about the expiration of accounts. Accounts
for students are ONLY given out at an instructor's request which could be
for NAY reason. I don't question faculty much (except for "needing" huge
amounts of disk space!).  That requirement alone has kept most hackers out.

The other things I do which annoy the hell out of students but seem to
work are:

  1.  REQUIRE a Novell login to gain access to a lab PC.
        After 3 failed attempts at logging in, the machine reboots.
        To prvent ctrl-C or ctrl-breaks, I turn the keyboard off many
        times during bootup using KBOFF.exe available by anonymous FTP
        at cs_dept_server_1.uhh.hawaii.edu.  Be sure to get the KBON.exe
        too since even ctrl-alt-del won't work either.

  2.  On Logout, if the volume name of C: drive is a "lab" name, I
        call reboot again to be sure 1. above is still in effect.  I need
        to work on login as well since some students figured out they could
        login (bogus-name) and have C: present. (I think I just figured out
        this one too since login.exe ought to return a code for a failed
        login, if the volume name also shows a lab machine, I can call
        reboot again.)

  3.  I made command.com look for something else besides autoexec.bat!  This
        one fools most game install programs! I also collect the account names
        of people who change the autoexec.bat and config.sys files, thus I can
        and will by the Fall, remove there accounts too!

  4.  The thing that makes 3 above work is a program we call diskfix.exe (not
        the PC tools one) we wrote to check the C: drive on bootup against
        a network template. Be sure to give readonly permission to everyone
        on the template subdirectory to keep fools and thieves from screwing
        things up!  This removes added software and replaces
        modified/missing files (like autoexec.bat, etc.) on very boot up and
        since we force reboots often, we have fairly clean drives.

        One MUST run something like the PC Tools Diskfix.exe to repair the C:
        drives though. We do this about 4 times per year per machine. This
        is not network specific but does affect the ability of the
        workstations to function.

You cannot be responsible for students being stupid.  If they forget to
log out, revoke their accounts too.  Our Dean is computer and
network literate and will back us up.  Our library PC lab is standalone and
is a MAJOR source of viruses. Thus:

   5.  On bootup, we check for viruses too. If a machine is infected, a
       text file is updated as a log entry and the machine is locked up. We
       display an "I am sick" message and run random sounds out the  speaker.
       NETSHLD.NLM also detected a user running infected freeware games. I
       also "admonished" him and warned further virus attacks would result
       in losing his account. We provide virus checking via a DougMenu option
       so ALL students have reasonable access to detect viruses before
       executing their software, thus we have little sympathy for the "I
       didn't know!" types.  One instructor,as a first PC assignment requires
       students to run the virus checker, print screen the results and
       turn these results in.  He gives a 0 (really hard core) for a lab
       assignment turned in with a virus on it. Those 2 things have
       eliminated his virus problems (at least from students!).


Let me know if you would like to see our typical autoexec.bat and
config.sys files too.

We also change our system often enough to keep people off guard. Users
basically have little idea what is happening even AFTER we catch them
screwing up.  I also have good working relations with other system
administrators on our island (Hawaii) and with our Manoa campus computing
center (CC), thus we all help each other out.  I really appreciate the
help our Manoa CC offers the outer campuses (a little horn blowing here!).

Well, gotta go! If you have any questions please subject them as "Security
questions" so I will not delete them as e-mail chaff!!!!!

