Donald Edwards <warrl@connected.com> wrote:
>Is there anything immoral about assigning each site administrator the
>responsibility of policing his own site?  It seems to me that this is
>inherent in the job of a site administrator.  In general, nobody *can*
>take action directly against an offending individual, save only the
>site administrator; so clearly the responsibility can reside nowhere else.

Wrong!  The only person who should "take action directly against" an
offending individual is the *appropriate authority at that site*.  In
some cases - like the typical *.com site - it may very well be the
system administrator, or their supervisor; at the typical *.edu site, 
it should *never* be the system administrator.  I'm a system admini-
strator; I have no desire whatsoever to be a judge.  That's why we 
have a Dean of Students.

>This means that if a problem persists, it is because a site administrator
>is not doing his job.  

Could it - just possibly - be that the system administrator doesn't 
see it as a problem?  I just went through this recently, when the
high administration of this university received complaints about 
"racist postings" from a user on this campus.  When they came to
me and said "What are we going to do?," my response was "absolutely
nothing" - and I made it stick.  I didn't agree with (or approve of)
the things that user said, but he said them within the context of
discussion in a limited public forum; there was no reason to take
action.

By the way, when did you write their job description?  If you can
say that he's "not doing his job," then you know what his job is
supposed to be, right?  Remember, he's a *SITE* or *SYSTEMS* ad-
ministrator, not a *USER* administrator.

>The only way the net populace has to discipline the site administrator 
>is via masses of mail sufficient to cause him problems with his systems. 

"discipline the site administrator"?  That's *got* to be the funniest
thing I've read in this newsgroup in quite some time...

"I think it's a problem, and you don't, so I'm going to *create* a prob-
lem for you!"  Geez, that's a *marvelous* viewpoint.  Who, may I ask (and 
please, be specific) put you in a position to *discipline* any system 
administrator?  It seems to me that you're assuming the very level of
authority for which so many system administrators are castigated.

>Sometimes
>a site administrator will be concerned about offenses by his users and
>try to stop them; sometimes he won't.  *One* note has to get to somebody
>who gives a shit; several thousand notes will compel someone (the site
>administrator) to care.

"Several thousand notes" - or even several dozen - from the same person
will compel this system administrator to care enough to file formal char-
ges against you, at *your* site, for intentional disruption of my site's 
operations.  *One* carefully written, thoughful, logical message, on the
other hand, will go much further toward convincing me...

>If anyone thinks this kind of response is wrong... tell us what other means
>exist to deal with troublemakers.

If the problem is one of opinion - you don't like what they say, but
it's within the topic under discussion - your only recourse is to ig-
nore the 'troublemakers.'

If the problem is one of *method* - spamming, email bombs, or the 
like - a *calm, rational, single* notice to the system administra-
tor may be in order.  (assuming, of course, that you have already
exhausted the normal, civil means of dealing with the problem your-
self.)

If you honestly believe that you are being harassed or discriminated
against, you are quite welcome to file charges against the other per-
son with the appropriate authority at their site.  At this university,
that means contacting the Dean of Students or Human Resources office.

Before you expect so much of other system admins, ask yourself if
you want *YOUR* admin to respond, in a similar "loose cannon" fas-
hion, to whatever flows into his mailbox.  

