longo@undergrad.csee.usf.edu (Andrea Longo) writes:

>I've gotten the University of Florida user policy; it is entirely too long to
>type in but I'll note important sections.

>=======quotes begine here======
>from:
>Policies for use of CIRCA computers, May 20 1994

>You have a right to expect that your files and electronic messages are
>reasonably private, however you must be aware that computer systems in an
>education environment are not always secure and your files and electronic mail
>may be seen by others.

>CIRCA has the right to monitor all activity on a computer system including
>individual sessions. 

First, state agents don't have any "rights". What may have is
"authority".  State agents, don't however, have authority, however,
to violate the constraints of the 4th Amendment of the U.S. Constitution
or of federal statute.

A U.S. government task force [see refs] says that "[Email] monitoring [of
government employees] of actual communications and communicators may
impinge on the Constitutional rights of freedom of speech (1st
Amendment), against unreasonable search and seizure (4th Amendment),
and against self-incrimination (5th amendment), as well as on the
right to privacy, specifically as set forth in both the Privacy Act
and the ECPA."

According to Mike Godwin, legal services counsel for the Electronic
Frontier Foundation (EFF), the U.S.'s Electronic Communications
Privacy Act (ECPA) could be reasonably construed to protect university
email. This is also the reported opinion of the U. of Michigan's
lawyers. Also, the U.S.'s Family Educational Rights and Privacy Act
gives students at all public and most private schools some privacy
rights.

>CIRCA computers are connected to world-wide computer networks. You must at all
>times comply with the conventions and rules for use of any computer network.
>CIRCA policy requires that you must:
>	not use electronic mail or messaging programs to harass or interfere
>	with other computer users, send or post obscene or defamatory
>	material, send excessive mail and chain letters.

Every use of a computer system "interferes" with someone else's use. I
suggest the the use of the phrase "substantially disrupt" for
"interferes". Also the word "obscene" is unclear to many people.  I
suggest referring to the Florida statute that defines obscenity or at
least adding "as defined by Florida statute".


>	not use any CIRCA facilities to make unauthorized connections or break
>	into or affect the performance of any other computer system on the
>	network, whether it is on the UF campus or anywhere else in the world.

Every use of a computer systems "effects" its "performance". This rule
outlaws all computer use.

>	not deliberately obscure your identity when sending electronic mail or
>	messages.

This rule makes it a punishable offense to post to alt.sexual-abuse.recovery
under a pseudonym.

There is nothing wrong with anonymity when it is not used to commit
fraud. As the Supreme Court said in _Tally v. California_:
 
  "Anonymously pamphlets, leaflets, brochures and even books
   have played an important role in the progress of mankind.
   Persecuted groups and sects from time to time throughout history
   have been able to criticize oppressive practices and laws either
   anonymously or not at all."
       _Tally v. California_, 362 U.S. 60, 64 (1960); see also _Wilson v.
       Stocker_, 819 F2d 943 (10th Cir. 1987)

_Tally v. California_, 362 U.S. 60, 64 (1960); see also _Wilson v.
Stocker_, 819 F2d 943 (10th Cir. 1987)


>	not conduct any commercial activity.

>=============================================================
>This thing is two pages of small type and covers a whole lot of possible
>activities, lots more detail than the USF one. 

